hello and welcome to the cheeky science  show so the last time i spoke about the  coronavirus  i said this i said to myself that i  wasn't going to do  another video on the coronavirus because  mainly reading all the news  pretty much terrified me and so i'm  still pretty terrified  but on this channel i like to talk about  science and there's a lot of really cool  science  and research coming out on the  coronavirus  and so one particular article caught my  eye  and say we're going to talk about it in  this video and so what this article was  trying to address  was why certain populations such as the  elderly are more vulnerable to  coronavirus infection  and it relates this vulnerability to a  mechanogenomic regulation  so in this video we'll talk all about  that and my interpretation of this  article  which actually isn't really an article  it's a comment so there's no  like recent data but it discusses  previous data and  comes up with its own hypothesis to  explain this but firstly let's just  describe what the coronavirus is  so coronaviruses belong to a family of  positive strands  rna viruses and through infection of  their hosts they can cause  upper respiratory tract illnesses such  as odds which is the acute respiratory  distress syndrome and so sars cough  ii is the coronavirus that is currently  causing this current pandemic  of the covered 19 and so what we've  unfortunately witnessed in this most  recent outbreak  is that coronaviruses are potentially  highly  infectious and not only is it highly  infectious but it also can have  considerable  fatality rates as we've also evidently  seen  however these fatality rates are  particularly high  in high-risk groups that include the  elderly and  others with underlying chronic medical  conditions  and this has a huge impact on the  economy and poses a really great burden  on the healthcare services  but the question is is why why do they  have such a high risk of  infection and then fatality  so currently the main hypothesis for why  this would be the case  is that these high risk groups um  have a dysfunction of the immune system  which makes them more susceptible to  infection  and then their ability to deal with the  situation but what was described in this  comment article  is an alternative hypothesis or one that  could also go  in combination which is an altered  cellular mechanical state  and so what this refers to is the  differences in the cell's response to  their surrounding environment  so to give an analogy let's say i'm  surrounded by two sponges that are  moving  towards me maybe not too bad a situation  but put two concrete walls besides me  that i'm even closer together  and i might not be too happy so you see  my response has changed because the  environment is different  and as strange as that analogy probably  was the same can be said for cells  cells reside in tissue and they're  surrounded by other cells but they're  also surrounded by  an extracellular matrix and cells need  to be able to  interact with and respond to changes in  its environment  to be able to survive and to help  prevent the cell from going broke  and causing cancers so the point i'm  trying to make is that cells effectively  talk to their environment  and their surroundings but not only do  they do this by  chemical signals but they can also  interact and communicate through  mechanical signals and it's the  conversion of mechanical signals to  biochemical signals that can propagate  within the cell  is referred to as mechanotransduction so  i'm planning on doing a future video  where i go into a bit more detail about  mechanic transduction because it is  really interesting and i've read a lot  about it recently regarding  an overlap with my phd project but for  now i'll just give a kind of  generic overview to what really is  mechanic transduction  and what is meant by mechanogenomic  regulation  so what i'm drawing out here is the  extracellular matrix  which i said is what surrounds these  cells and so then i've drawn out the  cell membrane  and so this is the barrier to the cell  and then what i've drawn on the bottom  here is the cell nucleus which contains  the dna  that contains the different genes that  are regulated and expressed within a  cell  and so what you can see is at the  membrane there are certain receptor  proteins that can actually interact with  the ecm the extracellular matrix  and through a mechanism that i won't  describe here it can also  interact through to the nucleus and  transmit signals that can  alter the gene expression patterns and  so  these gene expression changes the cell's  responses  can differ depending on the stiffness of  the ecm such as in my analogy  between the sponge and concrete so what  does this have to do with the  coronavirus  well if we think about what the  coronavirus wants to do  it's programmed to make more copies of  itself  and to make more copies of itself it  exploits its host  and it so therefore it exploits the  machinery that's  in the human cells but critical to its  success it wants to do this such that  it's undetected  and so previous work and i just want to  point out here that the references i've  used i've put  the links to the papers in the  description box  have emphasized the use of the nf kappa  b pathway  in this process so what is the nf kappa  b  process well nf cavity is a protein and  it does a lot of things in the cell but  one thing that it does is it  can promote an inflammatory response and  so this response can bring in the immune  cells that can then destroy and kill  infected cells  but studies have also shown that this  protein  and its response can also be activated  by the coronavirus  to promote its replication but how would  this work if the coronavirus wants to  remain undetected  well as i said the n of kappa b pathway  does a variety of things  and so what is now thought is that the  chronovirus actually fine-tunes this  pathway  and so this means that it can still  replicate but still try and remain  undetected so what has this got to do  with age  well the coronavirus largely infects the  lungs and the respiratory system  in aging individuals is characterized by  fibroblast dysfunction  this can result in increased deposition  of the extracellular matrix  and increase the tissue stiffness and  going back to what we said earlier  cells respond differently when they're  witness to  different stiffnesses in their  surrounding environment  and one pathway that can be altered  through a different  stiffness of tissue is the nf kappa b  pathway  so does the virus exploit this such that  it can  infect cells that are in a stiffer  environment  so if you take some cells that are  growing on either a soft or a stiff  matrix and you expose them both to the  same  inflammatory signal you see that they  both activate the inner kappa b pathway  but they both have differences in the  downstream genes  from the nf kappa b that are expressed  and so just imagine that those different  genes are currently within  that purple circle now the interesting  part  is that if you also take a list of genes  that are differently expressed by  coronavirus infection  there's a substantial overlap between  these genes  so genes are expressed differently  depending on the mechanical states i.e  if it's soft or stiff could have some  interaction on impact on the coronavirus  infection  and so this overlap is actually quite  large and  therefore it's interesting to look at  these genes that are affected by both of  them  and to see if we can learn anything from  that so  it seems that most of these genes have  important immune modulatory effects  and so it could be that the chronovirus  can exploit the vulnerability of cells  in a stiffer environment  and improve their chances of replication  without the cells knowing that they're  there  so as i said this is currently just the  hypothesis but i think it could have  important implications not only for the  coronavirus but for understanding other  viral infections  so i hope you learned something and as  always thanks for listening
 

